<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Power rule: negative powers (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The power rule \( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(x^{n}\right) = n\,x^{\,n-1} \) works for <b>negative</b> powers as well as positive ones. Here is the pattern for positive powers:</p>
<table style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;">
<tr><th style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( y \)</th><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( x^{3} \)</td><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( x^{2} \)</td><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( x^{1} \)</td></tr>
<tr><th style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( \dfrac{dy}{dx} \)</th><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( 3x^{2} \)</td><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( 2x^{1} \)</td><td style="border:1px solid #000;padding:6px;">\( 1x^{0} \)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Each derivative has the form \( n\cdot x^{\,n-1} \): the <b>coefficient</b> is the old power \( n \), and the <b>new power</b> is \( n-1 \). This keeps working when \( n \) is negative. In each line below, first rewrite the function as a single power (the <b>index</b>), then fill in the coefficient and the new power.</p>
<div><b>For \( \dfrac{1}{x} \):</b> \( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(\dfrac{1}{x}\right) = \dfrac{d}{dx}\Bigl(x \)<sup>[[input:idx1]]</sup>\( \Bigr) = \) [[input:c1]] \( \cdot\; x \)<sup>[[input:p1]]</sup></div>
<div>[[validation:idx1]] [[validation:c1]] [[validation:p1]] [[feedback:prtidx1]] [[feedback:prtc1]] [[feedback:prtp1]]</div>
<p></p>
<div><b>For \( \dfrac{1}{x^{2}} \):</b> \( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\right) = \dfrac{d}{dx}\Bigl(x \)<sup>[[input:idx2]]</sup>\( \Bigr) = \) [[input:c2]] \( \cdot\; x \)<sup>[[input:p2]]</sup></div>
<div>[[validation:idx2]] [[validation:c2]] [[validation:p2]] [[feedback:prtidx2]] [[feedback:prtc2]] [[feedback:prtp2]]</div>
<hr>
<p> Now let's applying this to \( f(x) = {@fA@} \):</p>
<div>\( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left({@fA@}\right) = \dfrac{d}{dx}\Bigl({@a@}\,x \)<sup>[[input:idxA]]</sup>\( \Bigr) = \) [[input:cA]] \( \cdot\; x \)<sup>[[input:pA]]</sup></div>
<div>[[validation:idxA]] [[validation:cA]] [[validation:pA]] [[feedback:prtidxA]] [[feedback:prtcA]] [[feedback:prtpA]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Write each function as a single power first, then apply \( n\,x^{\,n-1} \).</p>
<p>\( \dfrac{1}{x} = x^{-1} \): so \(n = -1 \), \(n-1 = -2 \), thus \( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(\tfrac{1}{x}\right) = \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(x^{-1}\right) = -x^{-2} \).</p>
<p>\( \dfrac{1}{x^{2}} = x^{-2} \): so \(n = -2 \), \(n-1 = -3 \), thus \( \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(\tfrac{1}{x^{2}}\right) = \dfrac{d}{dx}\!\left(x^{-2}\right) = -2x^{-3} \).</p>
<p>\( {@fA@} = {@a@}\,x^{ {#idxA_ta#} } \). Bring the power down and multiply by the coefficient: \( {@a@}\cdot({#idxA_ta#}) = {@cA_ta@} \); reduce the power to \( {@pA_ta@} \). So</p>
\[ f'(x) = {@dA@}. \]
<p>Watch: the constant multiple \( {@a@} \) stays in front, and subtracting \( 1 \) from a <em>negative</em> power makes it <em>more</em> negative.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
